{"language_code":"eng","language_probability":0.9765604138374329,"text":"The Leaning Tower of Pisa is only still standing because its city went to war. It's 1173, and Pisa is rich, proud, and building the fanciest bell tower in Italy. They dig a foundation just three meters deep, right into soft, wet clay. Floor one goes up, perfect. Floor two goes up, still perfect. Floor three goes up, and the whole tower quietly leans over. Luckily, Pisa then got dragged into a war. Unluckily for the builders, it lasted almost 100 years, and that's what saved it. Soft clay is full of water. Ever stood still on wet sand at the beach? You sink a little, then the water squeezes out, and the sand holds you. A century of waiting let the clay firm up under all that stone. When the builders came back, they made the new floors taller on the low side to hide the tilt. So now, the tower isn't just leaning, it's bent like a banana. In 1934, Mussolini ordered it straightened. His engineers pumped tons of concrete into the base, and the tower leaned even further. By 1990, it was so close to falling that Italy closed it. Engineers then spent 11 years gently pulling soil out from under the high side, and the tower sank back about 40 centimeters. They could have kept going until it stood perfectly straight. They stopped on purpose. Nobody flies to Italy to photograph a tower that stands up.","words":[{"text":"The","start":0.18,"end":0.22,"type":"word","logprob":-0.000014781842764932662},{"text":" ","start":0.22,"end":0.26,"type":"spacing","logprob":-0.018157219514250755},{"text":"Leaning","start":0.26,"end":0.44,"type":"word","logprob":-0.005188117601960585},{"text":" ","start":0.44,"end":0.52,"type":"spacing","logprob":-9.298280929215252e-6},{"text":"Tower","start":0.52,"end":0.7,"type":"word","logprob":-9.298280929215252e-6},{"text":" ","start":0.7,"end":0.74,"type":"spacing","logprob":-4.768370445162873e-7},{"text":"of","start":0.74,"end":0.8,"type":"word","logprob":-4.768370445162873e-7},{"t